375, 0.00263214111328125, 0.01788330078125, 0.01812744140625, 0.0073394775390625, -0.01136016845703125, 0.0098876953125, 0.01181793212890625, -0.02093505859375, -0.006107330322265625, -0.034332275390625, -0.0105133056640625, -0.0254974365234375, -0.003551483154296875, 0.01215362548828125, -0.0071563720703125, -0.009429931640625, 0.0014410018920898438, 0.00542449951171875, -0.005733489990234375, -0.00771331787109375, 0.00450897216796875, 0.03973388671875, -0.04693603515625, -0.0130157470703125, 0.0009407997131347656, 0.0128936767578125, -0.01013946533203125, 0.01117706298828125, 0.00571441650390625, -0.0021114349365234375, 0.00891876220703125, -0.048065185546875, -0.0162811279296875, -0.0092926025390625, -0.0112152099609375, 0.0264129638671875, -0.0285797119140625, 0.0234222412109375, -0.04864501953125, -0.0026264190673828125, -0.0233154296875, -0.01491546630859375, -0.0004367828369140625, -0.0120391845703125, -0.00962066650390625, 0.0087890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model to include fixed effects, allowing for both structured covariate information and flexible nonparametric modeling within a unified framework. Specifically, we introduce a prior distribution on the fixed effects coefficients and analyze the model in the diffuse prior limit, where the prior becomes increasingly non-informative and the coefficients are effectively estimated from the data. This limit is important because it leads to a decomposition of the intensity function that separates the contribution of fixed effects from the nonparametric component.\n\nIn this setting, we demonstrate that the intensity function of the permanental process can be characterized using the representer theorem, a result from functional analysis which states that the solution to certain regularized optimization problems in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) can be expressed as a finite linear combination of kernel functions evaluated at the data points. Here, the RKHS is a space of functions associated with a positive definite kernel, and the equivalent kernel in the limiting case defines the structure of this space. We show that the squared norm in this RKHS corresponds exactly to the limiting penalty term in the model, providing a direct connection between the statistical regularization and the geometry of the function space.\n\nThis decomposition has important implications for interpretation and practical modeling. By isolating the fixed effects term, practitioners can directly assess the impact of known covariates, while the RKHS component captures complex, unknown structure in the data. For example, in ecological modeling, fixed effects might represent known environmental variables, while the RKHS term models spatial variation. This structure also facilitates the incorporation of domain knowledge, as prior information about covariate effects or kernel choice can be encoded directly into the model.\n\nIn summary, our extension enables more interpretable and flexible permanental process models, broadening their applicability to scientific domains where both fixed and nonparametric effects are relevant. Potential applications include spatial statistics, survival analysis, and any setting where point process intensity functions must account for both known and unknown sources of variation."}}